# Budera — product context (informational)

**Budera** is **“the bank account for AI agents.”** It is **not** a generic payment gateway or neobank for humans alone. It is a **persistent, programmable financial identity at the API layer** so **non-human principals** (agents) can hold, receive, and move money under **rules humans approve**.

**Core idea:** Payment rails today assume **human** actors. Agents now book travel, pay invoices, and run spend — but they need **wallet + ledger + controls + audit** built for **agents + their operators**, not a one-off Stripe integration.

**Three product layers (conceptual):**

- **Identity** — Each agent (or product) gets a **wallet** / financial identity; stable across sessions; often tied to an **AI company** tenant and a **human end user** who authorizes.
- **Funding** — humans **link external bank** once; funds move to agent wallets on demand or schedule.
- **Spend controls** — programmable limits, categories, approvals, velocity; **full audit** of what the agent did.

**Who touches the product (actors):**

- **AI companies** — integrate via API, manage keys, policies, webhooks.
- **Human end users** — authorize agents, link bank, set limits, revoke access.
- **Agents** — act via **API credentials** (not human logins); every money movement is auditable.
- **Budera internal** — ops, risk, **live** access approvals.
- **Bank / sponsor partners** — KYB, compliance, reporting (read-heavy portals).

**Sandbox vs live:** Defaults are **sandbox**; **live** money and keys are **approval-gated** — see `docs/tech-stack.md`.

**Depth:** `docs/budera-strategy-full.md` (strategy), `docs/budera-dev-timeline.md` (design-first build plan), `docs/tech-stack.md` (stack decisions).

When suggesting features or code, align with **money safety**, **tenant isolation**, **auditability**, and **agent vs human** boundaries — not generic CRUD.
